      </div>

      <div class="dl-usecases-grid">
        <article class="dl-usecase-card">
          <h3 class="dl-usecase-title">"The video won't download."</h3>
          <p class="dl-usecase-body">
            Nine times out of ten, this means the TikTok link is wrong. Someone copied a link from a comment thread instead of the actual video, or the URL got mangled during a copy-paste. Go back to TikTok, open the video itself, tap Share → Copy Link, and paste that fresh URL. If the video was deleted by the creator, no downloader can bring it back — not TikSaveHub, not anyone else.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article class="dl-usecase-card">
          <h3 class="dl-usecase-title">"The quality is low."</h3>
          <p class="dl-usecase-body">
            Quality depends entirely on what the creator exported and uploaded. TikSaveHub can't improve a 480p video into 1080p. Check the video on TikTok first — if it looks sharp there, TikSaveHub delivers the same thing. If it's fuzzy on TikTok, it'll be fuzzy in the download too. That's a source-quality issue, not a tool limitation.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article class="dl-usecase-card">
          <h3 class="dl-usecase-title">"The link isn't working."</h3>
          <p class="dl-usecase-body">
            TikSaveHub handles all standard TikTok link formats, but it only works with video URLs. Profile pages, hashtag pages, and search result URLs aren't downloadable — they don't point to a specific video. The link needs to contain /video/, vm.tiktok.com, or vt.tiktok.com. If it's a profile link (tiktok.com/@username without /video/), there's no video to fetch.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article class="dl-usecase-card">
          <h3 class="dl-usecase-title">"Download takes too long."</h3>
          <p class="dl-usecase-body">
            The processing itself is fast — under three seconds. The bottleneck is file transfer. A 60-second 1080p TikTok video runs about 8 to 15MB. On a 10 Mbps connection, that's roughly 8 to 12 seconds of actual download time. On slower mobile data, it takes longer. Try the SD option for a smaller file, or switch to Wi-Fi.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article class="dl-usecase-card">
          <h3 class="dl-usecase-title">"The watermark is still there."</h3>
          <p class="dl-usecase-body">
            Rare, but it happens — usually when TikSaveHub can't locate the original stream and falls back to the in-app version. Wait a few minutes and try again. If the problem sticks around, the video may have been restricted on TikTok's end. This isn't common, but it's worth knowing about.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>
